No. `INSERT_IF_ABSENT` rejects a conflicting root (`isAccepted() == false`) and does **not** return the existing row, even with `execute(ItemView.class)`.

```java
var result = sqlClient.saveCommand(input)
    .setMode(SaveMode.INSERT_IF_ABSENT)
    .execute(ItemView.class);

if (result.isAccepted()) {
    return result.getModifiedView();
}
return itemRepository.findBySku(input.getSku()); // explicit conflict lookup
```

The SKU must have a database unique constraint. The save and conflict lookup are separate statements, so use an appropriate transaction/isolation policy (and retry/handle a concurrent delete) if the returned existing ID must be concurrency-consistent.

```kotlin
val result = sql.save(incomingCatalogRevision) {
    setMode(SaveMode.UPSERT)
    setVersionMode(VersionMode.ASSIGNMENT)
    setUpdateWhere(CatalogRevision::class) {
        newNonNull(CatalogRevision::version) gt table.version
    }
}

if (result.isAccepted) {
    val saved = result.modifiedEntity
    // Consume the accepted revision.
} else {
    // Older or equal incoming revision: normal rejected result.
    // No optimistic-lock exception is thrown.
}
```

`VersionMode.ASSIGNMENT` writes the supplied external version without implicit incrementing or optimistic-lock checking. The `setUpdateWhere` predicate applies only to the update branch: missing IDs are inserted, while an existing row is updated only when the incoming `version` is greater than the stored version.

The version mode is root-only; it is not propagated to associated child objects. Configure child saves separately if they also use externally assigned versions.

Put `@OnDissociate(DissociateAction.DELETE)` on the owning, non-null `Bin.warehouse()` association—not on `Warehouse.bins()`:

```java
// Warehouse
@OneToMany(mappedBy = "warehouse")
List<Bin> bins();
```

```java
// Bin
@ManyToOne
@OnDissociate(DissociateAction.DELETE)
Warehouse warehouse();
```

When a saved `Warehouse` replaces its `bins` collection, Jimmer treats removed bins as dissociated and deletes those `Bin` rows through ORM operations. The existing database FK may remain `RESTRICT`; it does not need to become `ON DELETE CASCADE`.

These are separate behaviors:

- `@OnDissociate(DELETE)` controls Jimmer’s handling of child removal/dissociation.
- `ON DELETE CASCADE` controls what the database does when the referenced `Warehouse` row itself is physically deleted.

Thus, change the FK only if database-level cascading on warehouse deletion is independently required.

```dto
export com.example.inventory.Shelf

dynamic input ShelfPatch {
    id
    note?
    bins? {
        id
    }
}
```

JSON round-trip assertion: deserialize `{"id":"…","note":null,"bins":[]}` and assert `toEntity()` has both `note` and `bins` loaded, with `note == null` and `bins` empty; omitting either field must leave it unloaded.

For Jimmer 0.12.2, map the hierarchy with `SINGLE_TABLE` and one discriminator:

```java
@Entity
@Inheritance(strategy = InheritanceType.SINGLE_TABLE)
public interface Asset {
    @Id long id();
    @Discriminator String kind();
    String name();
}

@Entity
@DiscriminatorValue("BOOK")
public interface BookAsset extends Asset {
    String isbn();
}

@Entity
@DiscriminatorValue("TOOL")
public interface ToolAsset extends Asset {
    int inspectionInterval();
}
```

`kind` is read-only: Jimmer derives it from the runtime entity subtype. The `SINGLE_TABLE` table contains columns for every subtype, so branch-only columns such as `isbn` and `inspection_interval` must be database-nullable, even though their subtype properties are non-null in the entity interfaces.

A root DTO can expose subtype-specific fields using type branches:

```text
export com.example.catalog.Asset

AssetView {
    id
    name
    #types {
        #exhaustive
        BookAsset {
            isbn
        }
        ToolAsset {
            inspectionInterval
        }
    }
}
```

`#exhaustive` makes DTO compilation fail when a concrete subtype is omitted. Without it, omitted types use the implicit common-fields branch.

No. Writing another discriminator value cannot switch an existing ID to a different subtype. The discriminator is not an assignable property. Type changes are disabled by default and require an explicitly enabled, exact subtype save (`setTypeChangeAllowed(true)`); do not use discriminator assignment as a transition mechanism.

```kotlin
val result = sqlClient.save(incomingCatalogRevision) {
    setMode(SaveMode.UPSERT)
    setVersionMode(VersionMode.ASSIGNMENT)

    // Update only when the incoming external revision is newer.
    setUpdateWhere(CatalogRevision::class) {
        newNonNull(CatalogRevision::version) gt table.version
    }
}

if (result.isAccepted) {
    val saved = result.modifiedEntity
    // Process the inserted row or newer revision.
} else {
    // Normal rejection: the row was missing from the update condition
    // because its stored version is equal to or newer.
}
```

`VersionMode.ASSIGNMENT` writes the supplied external version directly; it does not perform optimistic-lock checking or incrementing. Therefore, an older revision is rejected normally (`isAccepted == false`) rather than raising an optimistic-lock exception.

The update condition applies only to the update branch: a missing ID is still inserted. The condition compares local physical columns and does not suppress insertion.

Version mode is **root-only**. It is not propagated to child or associated objects; configure their save behavior separately if needed.

Put `@OnDissociate(DissociateAction.DELETE)` on the owning, non-null child reference—not on the inverse collection:

```java
interface Warehouse {
    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "warehouse")
    List<Bin> bins();
}

interface Bin {
    @ManyToOne
    @OnDissociate(DissociateAction.DELETE)
    Warehouse warehouse();
}
```

When a replacement save removes a `Bin` from `Warehouse.bins`, Jimmer dissociates that child and issues a `DELETE` for the `Bin` row. This is ORM behavior and does not require database `ON DELETE CASCADE`.

You do **not** need to change the existing `RESTRICT` foreign key. That FK controls what happens when the referenced `Warehouse` itself is deleted; `RESTRICT` can remain appropriate. Database cascade behavior is separate and does not replace the Jimmer dissociation policy.

